# Every sentence of RFC 8446 (rfc8446.txt, next to this file) that holds MUST,
# SHALL, REQUIRED, SHOULD or RECOMMENDED, and how shioko accounts for it.
#
#   @ <section>
#   > <the sentence>
#   = <status>      one or more; the kinds are described in tools/spec.sh,
#                   which also checks this file

@ 1.1
> The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
= info terminology: says how the requirement key words are to be read

@ 2
> If (EC)DHE key establishment is in use, then the ServerHello contains a "key_share" extension with the server's ephemeral Diffie-Hellman share; the server's share MUST be in the same group as one of the client's shares.
= code put exactly one KeyShareEntry in ServerHello, group x25519 (0x001D), and only in answer to a ClientHello that carried an x25519 share
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test a_hello_retry_request_and_then_the_handshake
= row step start whole even strict plain:hello_retry => retry whole even strict send:retry send:ccs
> Application Data MUST NOT be sent prior to sending the Finished message, except as specified in Section 2.3.
= check Handshake.DataOnlyAfterFinished
= check Handshake.DataOnlyWhenOpen
= row step wait_finished whole even strict write:- => wait_finished whole even strict
= row step wait_finished whole even strict protected:data => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message

@ 2.1
> If no common cryptographic parameters can be negotiated, the server MUST abort the handshake with an appropriate alert.
= row step start whole even strict invalid:handshake_failure => closed whole even strict alert:handshake_failure
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
= code answer a ClientHello without 0x0304 with protocol_version, and one that lacks TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, x25519 in supported_groups or ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 with handshake_failure
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test refused_hellos_get_the_alert_of_the_plan
= test bug_repeated_extension_hid_the_version

@ 2.2
> When a client offers resumption via a PSK, it SHOULD also supply a "key_share" extension to the server to allow the server to decline resumption and fall back to a full handshake, if needed.
= peer client: nothing to do; offered PSKs are ignored and a full handshake follows, and a ClientHello without key_share is refused with missing_extension (PLAN 5)
> When PSKs are provisioned out of band, the PSK identity and the KDF hash algorithm to be used with the PSK MUST also be provisioned.
= unused external PSK: no PSK can be configured; offered PSKs are ignored and a full handshake follows (PLAN 5)

@ 4
> Protocol messages MUST be sent in the order defined in Section 4.4.1 and shown in the diagrams in Section 2.
= check Handshake.InOrder
= row step start whole even strict plain:hello => wait_finished whole even strict send:server_hello send:ccs write_keys:handshake read_keys:handshake send:flight write_keys:data
= row step start whole even strict plain:hello_retry => retry whole even strict send:retry send:ccs
= row step retry whole even strict plain:hello => wait_finished whole even strict send:server_hello write_keys:handshake read_keys:handshake send:flight write_keys:data
= code encode the flight as EncryptedExtensions, Certificate, CertificateVerify, Finished, in that order
= test a_whole_handshake_and_data_both_ways
> A peer which receives a handshake message in an unexpected order MUST abort the handshake with an "unexpected_message" alert.
= row step start whole even strict plain:finished => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step start whole even strict plain:other => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step retry whole even strict plain:other => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step wait_finished whole even strict protected:hello => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step wait_finished whole even strict protected:key_update => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step wait_finished whole even strict protected:other => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step open whole even strict protected:finished => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step open whole even strict protected:other => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= code classify every handshake type other than client_hello, finished and key_update as "other"
= test other_handshake_messages_are_unexpected

@ 4.1.1
> If there is no overlap between the received "supported_groups" and the groups supported by the server, then the server MUST abort the handshake with a "handshake_failure" or an "insufficient_security" alert.
= row step start whole even strict invalid:handshake_failure => closed whole even strict alert:handshake_failure
= code reject a ClientHello whose supported_groups does not list x25519 with handshake_failure
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test refused_hellos_get_the_alert_of_the_plan
> If the server selects a PSK, then it MUST also select a key establishment mode from the set indicated by the client's "psk_key_exchange_modes" extension (at present, PSK alone or with (EC)DHE).
= unused PSK: shioko never selects a PSK; offered PSKs are ignored and a full handshake follows (PLAN 5)
> If the server selects an (EC)DHE group and the client did not offer a compatible "key_share" extension in the initial ClientHello, the server MUST respond with a HelloRetryRequest (Section 4.1.4) message.
= row step start whole even strict plain:hello_retry => retry whole even strict send:retry send:ccs
= code treat a ClientHello that lists x25519 in supported_groups and has a key_share extension without an x25519 entry as hello_retry, and answer with a HelloRetryRequest whose selected_group is x25519
= test a_hello_retry_request_and_then_the_handshake
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
> If the server is unable to negotiate a supported set of parameters (i.e., there is no overlap between the client and server parameters), it MUST abort the handshake with either a "handshake_failure" or "insufficient_security" fatal alert (see Section 6).
= row step start whole even strict invalid:handshake_failure => closed whole even strict alert:handshake_failure
= code abort with handshake_failure when the ClientHello offers TLS 1.3 but not TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, x25519 or ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test refused_hellos_get_the_alert_of_the_plan

@ 4.1.2
> When a client first connects to a server, it is REQUIRED to send the ClientHello as its first TLS message.
= peer client: any other first message is refused with unexpected_message
= row step start whole even strict plain:other => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step start whole even strict plain:finished => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step start whole even strict plain:data => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> In that case, the client MUST send the same ClientHello without modification, except as follows:
= peer client: the second ClientHello is parsed and judged afresh; one that still lacks the x25519 share or offers early data again is refused with illegal_parameter
= code refuse with illegal_parameter a second ClientHello that differs from the first in anything but key_share, early_data, pre_shared_key and padding: legacy_version, random, session id, suites, compression, and every other extension in order
= test bug_a_changed_second_client_hello_was_served
= row step retry whole even strict plain:hello_retry => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
= row step retry whole even strict plain:hello_early => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> Because TLS 1.3 forbids renegotiation, if a server has negotiated TLS 1.3 and receives a ClientHello at any other time, it MUST terminate the connection with an "unexpected_message" alert.
= row step wait_finished whole even strict protected:hello => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step wait_finished whole even strict plain:hello => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step open whole even strict protected:hello => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
= row step open whole even strict protected:hello_retry => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> If a server established a TLS connection with a previous version of TLS and receives a TLS 1.3 ClientHello in a renegotiation, it MUST retain the previous protocol version.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6); no pre-1.3 connection ever exists to renegotiate
> In particular, it MUST NOT negotiate TLS 1.3.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6); no pre-1.3 connection ever exists to renegotiate
> In TLS 1.3, the client indicates its version preferences in the "supported_versions" extension (Section 4.2.1) and the legacy_version field MUST be set to 0x0303, which is the version number for TLS 1.2.
= peer client: nothing to do; legacy_version takes no part in choosing the version when supported_versions is present (4.2.1)
> A client which has a cached session ID set by a pre-TLS 1.3 server SHOULD set this field to that value.
= peer client: nothing to do; the session id is only echoed (PLAN 5)
> In compatibility mode (see Appendix D.4), this field MUST be non-empty, so a client not offering a pre-TLS 1.3 session MUST generate a new 32-byte value.
= peer client: nothing to do; a legacy_session_id of any length from 0 to 32 is echoed
> This value need not be random but SHOULD be unpredictable to avoid implementations fixating on a specific value (also known as ossification).
= peer client: nothing to do
> Otherwise, it MUST be set as a zero-length vector (i.e., a zero-valued single byte length field).
= peer client: nothing to do; an empty session id is echoed empty
> If the list contains cipher suites that the server does not recognize, support, or wish to use, the server MUST ignore those cipher suites and process the remaining ones as usual.
= code search cipher_suites for 0x1303 and skip every other value, known or unknown; abort with handshake_failure only when 0x1303 is absent
= test a_modern_client_hello
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test unknown_values_are_ignored
> If the client is attempting a PSK key establishment, it SHOULD advertise at least one cipher suite indicating a Hash associated with the PSK.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko never selects a PSK
> For every TLS 1.3 ClientHello, this vector MUST contain exactly one byte, set to zero, which corresponds to the "null" compression method in prior versions of TLS.
= peer client: refused with illegal_parameter (next sentence)
> If a TLS 1.3 ClientHello is received with any other value in this field, the server MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= code reject a ClientHello that offers 0x0304 and whose legacy_compression_methods is not exactly one zero byte, with illegal_parameter; an empty list, below the vector's minimum length of 1, does not parse and gets decode_error
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test refused_hellos_get_the_alert_of_the_plan
= row step start whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> Note that TLS 1.3 servers might receive TLS 1.2 or prior ClientHellos which contain other compression methods and (if negotiating such a prior version) MUST follow the procedures for the appropriate prior version of TLS.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6), whatever its compression methods
> Servers MUST ignore unrecognized extensions.
= code skip every ClientHello extension whose type shioko does not act on, without error and without looking at its contents
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
= test unknown_values_are_ignored
> If negotiating a version of TLS prior to 1.3, a server MUST check that the message either contains no data after legacy_compression_methods or that it contains a valid extensions block with no data following.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6)
= code take a ClientHello that ends right after legacy_compression_methods for a pre-1.3 hello and answer protocol_version, not decode_error
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
> If not, then it MUST abort the handshake with a "decode_error" alert.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6)
= code reject a ClientHello whose extensions block is malformed or is followed by more bytes, with decode_error
= test bytes_past_the_extensions_or_an_extension
= row step start whole even strict invalid:decode_error => closed whole even strict alert:decode_error

@ 4.1.3
> In TLS 1.3, the TLS server indicates its version using the "supported_versions" extension (Section 4.2.1), and the legacy_version field MUST be set to 0x0303, which is the version number for TLS 1.2.
= code encode ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest with legacy_version 0x0303
= test our_messages_are_well_formed
> The last 8 bytes MUST be overwritten as described below if negotiating TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.1, but the remaining bytes MUST be random.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6); no downgrade marker is ever written
= code fill ServerHello.random with 32 fresh bytes from /dev/urandom for every handshake, and fail the handshake if they cannot be read
= test every_server_random_is_new
> This structure is generated by the server and MUST be generated independently of the ClientHello.random.
= code take ServerHello.random from /dev/urandom alone; no byte of the ClientHello enters it
= test every_server_random_is_new
> A client which receives a legacy_session_id_echo field that does not match what it sent in the ClientHello MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do
= code echo the ClientHello legacy_session_id byte for byte in ServerHello and in HelloRetryRequest
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test every_server_random_is_new
= test a_hello_retry_request_and_then_the_handshake
> A client which receives a cipher suite that was not offered MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko sends 0x1303 only after finding it in the client's list
> legacy_compression_method: A single byte which MUST have the value 0.
= code encode legacy_compression_method as 0 in ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
> The ServerHello MUST only include extensions which are required to establish the cryptographic context and negotiate the protocol version.
= code put exactly two extensions in ServerHello, supported_versions and key_share; answers to server_name and ALPN go in EncryptedExtensions
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test a_whole_handshake_and_data_both_ways
> All TLS 1.3 ServerHello messages MUST contain the "supported_versions" extension.
= code put supported_versions with selected_version 0x0304 in every ServerHello
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
> Upon receiving a message with type server_hello, implementations MUST first examine the Random value and, if it matches this value, process it as described in Section 4.1.4).
= peer client: nothing to do; a server_hello sent to shioko is "other" and ends in unexpected_message
= row step start whole even strict plain:other => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> TLS 1.3 servers which negotiate TLS 1.2 or below in response to a ClientHello MUST set the last 8 bytes of their Random value specially in their ServerHello.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6); no downgrade marker is ever written
> If negotiating TLS 1.2, TLS 1.3 servers MUST set the last 8 bytes of their Random value to the bytes:
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6); no downgrade marker is ever written
> If negotiating TLS 1.1 or below, TLS 1.3 servers MUST, and TLS 1.2 servers SHOULD, set the last 8 bytes of their ServerHello.Random value to the bytes:
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6); no downgrade marker is ever written
> TLS 1.3 clients receiving a ServerHello indicating TLS 1.2 or below MUST check that the last 8 bytes are not equal to either of these values.
= peer client: nothing to do
> TLS 1.2 clients SHOULD also check that the last 8 bytes are not equal to the second value if the ServerHello indicates TLS 1.1 or below.
= peer client: nothing to do
> If a match is found, the client MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do
> A legacy TLS client performing renegotiation with TLS 1.2 or prior and which receives a TLS 1.3 ServerHello during renegotiation MUST abort the handshake with a "protocol_version" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do

@ 4.1.4
> The server's extensions MUST contain "supported_versions".
= code put supported_versions with selected_version 0x0304 in the HelloRetryRequest
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
> Additionally, it SHOULD contain the minimal set of extensions necessary for the client to generate a correct ClientHello pair.
= code put exactly two extensions in the HelloRetryRequest: supported_versions and key_share with selected_group x25519
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
> As with the ServerHello, a HelloRetryRequest MUST NOT contain any extensions that were not first offered by the client in its ClientHello, with the exception of optionally the "cookie" (see Section 4.2.2) extension.
= code send a HelloRetryRequest only to a ClientHello that itself carries supported_versions and a key_share extension, and put nothing but supported_versions and key_share in it; a ClientHello without key_share gets missing_extension, and no cookie is ever sent (PLAN 5)
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= row step start whole even strict invalid:missing_extension => closed whole even strict alert:missing_extension
> Upon receipt of a HelloRetryRequest, the client MUST check the legacy_version, legacy_session_id_echo, cipher_suite, and legacy_compression_method as specified in Section 4.1.3 and then process the extensions, starting with determining the version using "supported_versions".
= peer client: nothing to do
> Clients MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert if the HelloRetryRequest would not result in any change in the ClientHello.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko asks for a retry only when the ClientHello has no x25519 share, so the request always changes it
> If a client receives a second HelloRetryRequest in the same connection (i.e., where the ClientHello was itself in response to a HelloRetryRequest), it MUST abort the handshake with an "unexpected_message" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko never sends a second HelloRetryRequest
= check Handshake.AtMostOnce
= row step retry whole even strict plain:hello_retry => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> Otherwise, the client MUST process all extensions in the HelloRetryRequest and send a second updated ClientHello.
= peer client: a client that sends no second ClientHello is ended at the 10 s handshake deadline with close_notify
= row step retry whole even strict close:- => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify
= check Handshake.HandshakeEnds
> A client which receives a cipher suite that was not offered MUST abort the handshake.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko sends 0x1303 only after finding it in the client's list
> Servers MUST ensure that they negotiate the same cipher suite when receiving a conformant updated ClientHello (if the server selects the cipher suite as the first step in the negotiation, then this will happen automatically).
= code write cipher suite 0x1303 in the HelloRetryRequest and in the ServerHello that follows, and refuse a second ClientHello that no longer offers 0x1303 with handshake_failure
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test a_second_client_hello_without_our_suite_fails
= row step retry whole even strict plain:hello => wait_finished whole even strict send:server_hello write_keys:handshake read_keys:handshake send:flight write_keys:data
> Upon receiving the ServerHello, clients MUST check that the cipher suite supplied in the ServerHello is the same as that in the HelloRetryRequest and otherwise abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do
> In addition, in its updated ClientHello, the client SHOULD NOT offer any pre-shared keys associated with a hash other than that of the selected cipher suite.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko never selects a PSK
> The value of selected_version in the HelloRetryRequest "supported_versions" extension MUST be retained in the ServerHello, and a client MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert if the value changes.
= code write selected_version 0x0304 in the HelloRetryRequest and in the ServerHello that follows
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= peer client: nothing to do

@ 4.2
> Implementations MUST NOT send extension responses if the remote endpoint did not send the corresponding extension requests, with the exception of the "cookie" extension in the HelloRetryRequest.
= code emit an extension in ServerHello, HelloRetryRequest or EncryptedExtensions only when the ClientHello carried the same type (supported_versions, key_share, server_name, ALPN); never send a cookie
= test without_alpn_none_is_chosen
= test our_messages_are_well_formed
> Upon receiving such an extension, an endpoint MUST abort the handshake with an "unsupported_extension" alert.
= unused extension requests from the server (CertificateRequest): shioko sends none, so no client message can carry an extension response; a client Certificate message is "other" and ends in unexpected_message
> If an implementation receives an extension which it recognizes and which is not specified for the message in which it appears, it MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= code act only on ClientHello extension types that the table allows in a ClientHello; skip every other type, oid_filters included, as unrecognized (the ClientHello is the only message with extensions that shioko accepts)
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
= test unknown_values_are_ignored
> +--------------------------------------------------+-------------+ | Extension | TLS 1.3 | +--------------------------------------------------+-------------+ | server_name [RFC6066] | CH, EE | | | | | max_fragment_length [RFC6066] | CH, EE | | | | | status_request [RFC6066] | CH, CR, CT | | | | | supported_groups [RFC7919] | CH, EE | | | | | signature_algorithms (RFC 8446) | CH, CR | | | | | use_srtp [RFC5764] | CH, EE | | | | | heartbeat [RFC6520] | CH, EE | | | | | application_layer_protocol_negotiation [RFC7301] | CH, EE | | | | | signed_certificate_timestamp [RFC6962] | CH, CR, CT | | | | | client_certificate_type [RFC7250] | CH, EE | | | | | server_certificate_type [RFC7250] | CH, EE | | | | | padding [RFC7685] | CH | | | | | key_share (RFC 8446) | CH, SH, HRR | | | | | pre_shared_key (RFC 8446) | CH, SH | | | | | psk_key_exchange_modes (RFC 8446) | CH | | | | | early_data (RFC 8446) | CH, EE, NST | | | | | cookie (RFC 8446) | CH, HRR | | | | | supported_versions (RFC 8446) | CH, SH, HRR | | | | | certificate_authorities (RFC 8446) | CH, CR | | | | | oid_filters (RFC 8446) | CR | | | | | post_handshake_auth (RFC 8446) | CH | | | | | signature_algorithms_cert (RFC 8446) | CH, CR | +--------------------------------------------------+-------------+ When multiple extensions of different types are present, the extensions MAY appear in any order, with the exception of "pre_shared_key" (Section 4.2.11) which MUST be the last extension in the ClientHello (but can appear anywhere in the ServerHello extensions block).
= peer client: a ClientHello with pre_shared_key anywhere but last is refused with illegal_parameter (PLAN 5)
= row step start whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> There MUST NOT be more than one extension of the same type in a given extension block.
= code never encode two extensions of the same type in ServerHello, HelloRetryRequest or EncryptedExtensions
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test our_messages_are_well_formed
= code reject a ClientHello that carries the same extension type twice, with illegal_parameter
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= row step start whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter

@ 4.2.1
> Implementations of this specification MUST send this extension in the ClientHello containing all versions of TLS which they are prepared to negotiate (for this specification, that means minimally 0x0304, but if previous versions of TLS are allowed to be negotiated, they MUST be present as well).
= peer client: a ClientHello without supported_versions is a pre-1.3 hello and is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6)
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> If this extension is not present, servers which are compliant with this specification and which also support TLS 1.2 MUST negotiate TLS 1.2 or prior as specified in [RFC5246], even if ClientHello.legacy_version is 0x0304 or later.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6)
> If this extension is present in the ClientHello, servers MUST NOT use the ClientHello.legacy_version value for version negotiation and MUST use only the "supported_versions" extension to determine client preferences.
= code when supported_versions is present, choose the version from it alone; legacy_version takes no part in the choice
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
> Servers MUST only select a version of TLS present in that extension and MUST ignore any unknown versions that are present in that extension.
= code select TLS 1.3 only when 0x0304 appears in supported_versions, and skip every other value in the list, unknown ones included
= test a_modern_client_hello
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test unknown_values_are_ignored
> Implementations of TLS 1.3 which choose to support prior versions of TLS SHOULD support TLS 1.2.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: shioko supports no earlier version at all, so the condition does not arise
> Servers MUST be prepared to receive ClientHellos that include this extension but do not include 0x0304 in the list of versions.
= code answer a ClientHello whose supported_versions does not contain 0x0304 with protocol_version
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test refused_hellos_get_the_alert_of_the_plan
= test bug_repeated_extension_hid_the_version
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> A server which negotiates a version of TLS prior to TLS 1.3 MUST set ServerHello.version and MUST NOT send the "supported_versions" extension.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer 0x0304 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN 2, change 6)
> A server which negotiates TLS 1.3 MUST respond by sending a "supported_versions" extension containing the selected version value (0x0304).
= code put supported_versions with selected_version 0x0304 in ServerHello
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
> It MUST set the ServerHello.legacy_version field to 0x0303 (TLS 1.2).
= code encode ServerHello.legacy_version as 0x0303
= test our_messages_are_well_formed
> Clients MUST check for this extension prior to processing the rest of the ServerHello (although they will have to parse the ServerHello in order to read the extension).
= peer client: nothing to do
> If this extension is present, clients MUST ignore the ServerHello.legacy_version value and MUST use only the "supported_versions" extension to determine the selected version.
= peer client: nothing to do
> If the "supported_versions" extension in the ServerHello contains a version not offered by the client or contains a version prior to TLS 1.3, the client MUST abort the handshake with an "illegal_parameter" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do

@ 4.2.2
> When sending the new ClientHello, the client MUST copy the contents of the extension received in the HelloRetryRequest into a "cookie" extension in the new ClientHello.
= unused cookie: shioko never puts a cookie in a HelloRetryRequest; a ClientHello that carries one is refused with illegal_parameter (PLAN 5)
= row step start whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
= row step retry whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> Clients MUST NOT use cookies in their initial ClientHello in subsequent connections.
= unused cookie: shioko never puts a cookie in a HelloRetryRequest; a ClientHello that carries one is refused with illegal_parameter (PLAN 5)
= row step start whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
= row step retry whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> Servers operating statelessly MUST ignore these records.
= unused stateless HelloRetryRequest: shioko keeps its state (phase retry) between the two ClientHellos; the ChangeCipherSpec that may come there is dropped all the same
= row step retry whole even strict plain:ccs => retry whole even strict

@ 4.2.3
> The keys found in certificates MUST also be of appropriate type for the signature algorithms they are used with.
