= row step open whole even strict plain:data => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message

@ 5.2
> The length MUST NOT exceed 2^14 + 256 bytes.
= code seal at most 2^14 bytes of content per record with no padding, so that TLSCiphertext.length never exceeds 2^14 + 17
= test large_data_goes_in_records_of_16k_at_most
= test our_records_have_no_padding
> An endpoint that receives a record that exceeds this length MUST terminate the connection with a "record_overflow" alert.
= row step open whole even strict invalid:record_overflow => closed whole even strict alert:record_overflow
= code reject a protected record whose length field exceeds 2^14 + 256 with record_overflow, before reading its body
= test records_are_framed_and_checked
> If the decryption fails, the receiver MUST terminate the connection with a "bad_record_mac" alert.
= row step open whole even strict undecryptable:- => closed whole even strict alert:bad_record_mac
= row step wait_finished whole even strict undecryptable:- => closed whole even strict alert:bad_record_mac
= check Handshake.SkipOnlyEarlyData
= code fail with bad_record_mac whenever AEAD opening of a record fails; the one exception is refused early data, which is skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB (PLAN section 5)
= test records_under_old_keys_are_bad_record_mac
= test early_data_is_skipped_up_to_a_limit
> An AEAD algorithm used in TLS 1.3 MUST NOT produce an expansion greater than 255 octets.
= info constraint on the AEAD algorithms admitted to TLS 1.3; the one suite used, ChaCha20-Poly1305, expands a record by 16 octets
> An endpoint that receives a record from its peer with TLSCiphertext.length larger than 2^14 + 256 octets MUST terminate the connection with a "record_overflow" alert.
= row step open whole even strict invalid:record_overflow => closed whole even strict alert:record_overflow
= code reject a protected record whose length field exceeds 2^14 + 256 with record_overflow, before reading its body
= test records_are_framed_and_checked

@ 5.3
> Each sequence number is set to zero at the beginning of a connection and whenever the key is changed; the first record transmitted under a particular traffic key MUST use sequence number 0.
= check Handshake.PeerCanDecrypt
= check Handshake.NoNonceReuse
= code start the read sequence number at 0 for every new read key (handshake, application, each KeyUpdate) and add one per record opened; the model tracks only the write side
= test a_whole_handshake_and_data_both_ways
= test key_updates_from_the_client
> If a TLS implementation would need to wrap a sequence number, it MUST either rekey (Section 4.6.3) or terminate the connection.
= code end the connection instead of sealing or opening a record once the sequence number of that key would wrap past 2^64 - 1; no sequence number is ever used twice (PLAN section 5: a sequence number that would wrap ends the connection)
= test sequence_numbers_do_not_wrap
= test bug_internal_failures_ended_silently
> An AEAD algorithm where N_MAX is less than 8 bytes MUST NOT be used with TLS.
= info constraint on which AEAD algorithms may be used with TLS; ChaCha20-Poly1305, the one suite used, takes a 12-byte nonce

@ 5.4
> Implementations MUST set the padding octets to all zeros before encrypting.
= code seal every record without padding: the inner plaintext is the content followed by the one content-type byte and nothing else
= test our_records_have_no_padding
> Implementations MUST NOT send Handshake and Alert records that have a zero-length TLSInnerPlaintext.content; if such a message is received, the receiving implementation MUST terminate the connection with an "unexpected_message" alert.
= code never seal a Handshake or Alert record with empty content
= test an_empty_write_sends_nothing
= code reject a decrypted Handshake or Alert record whose content is empty with unexpected_message
= test bug_empty_alert_got_the_wrong_alert
= test a_client_hello_in_pieces_and_how_not_to
= row step open whole even strict invalid:unexpected_message => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> Implementations MUST limit their scanning to the cleartext returned from the AEAD decryption.
= code find the content type by scanning back from the end of the AEAD output only (Vec::last and Vec::pop on the decrypted bytes, no indexing), never past the start of the cleartext
= test padding_is_stripped_and_content_must_have_a_type
> If a receiving implementation does not find a non-zero octet in the cleartext, it MUST terminate the connection with an "unexpected_message" alert.
= code reject a decrypted record that is empty or all zero octets with unexpected_message
= test padding_is_stripped_and_content_must_have_a_type
= row step open whole even strict invalid:unexpected_message => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> The presence of padding does not change the overall record size limitations: the full encoded TLSInnerPlaintext MUST NOT exceed 2^14 + 1 octets.
= code split outgoing application data and our own handshake messages so that no record carries more than 2^14 octets of content (PLAN section 5)
= test large_data_goes_in_records_of_16k_at_most
= test a_flight_larger_than_a_record
= code reject a record whose AEAD output (content, type and padding together) exceeds 2^14 + 1 octets with record_overflow
= test bug_padding_did_not_count_towards_the_limit
= test padding_is_stripped_and_content_must_have_a_type
= row step open whole even strict invalid:record_overflow => closed whole even strict alert:record_overflow

@ 5.5
> Implementations SHOULD do a key update as described in Section 4.6.3 prior to reaching these limits.
= info no usage limit is reached before the sequence number wraps: the RFC says so for ChaCha20-Poly1305, the only suite here, so no limit-driven KeyUpdate arises; a sequence number that would wrap ends the connection (5.3 entry above)

@ 6
> Upon receiving such an alert, the TLS implementation SHOULD indicate end-of-data to the application.
= row step open whole even strict protected:close_notify => closed whole even strict alert:close_notify
= code after a received close_notify, reads on the TLS stream return end-of-data (Ok(0)), not an error
= test bug_injected_close_notify_looked_like_an_orderly_end
= test bug_error_alerts_looked_like_a_clean_close
= test alerts_from_the_client
