= code abort the handshake with illegal_parameter when the X25519 shared secret is 32 zero bytes, and test it with low-order points (tests/vectors/x25519.txt holds vectors with an all-zero result, among them u = 0 and u = 1)
= test x25519_rfc7748
= test wycheproof_x25519
= test an_all_zero_share_is_illegal
= row step start whole even strict invalid:illegal_parameter => closed whole even strict alert:illegal_parameter
> If implementors use an alternative implementation of these elliptic curves, they SHOULD perform the additional checks specified in Section 7 of [RFC7748].
= unused an alternative implementation of the curves: X25519 is the RFC 7748 ladder from libcrux-curve25519 (PLAN section 7), so the extra checks of RFC 7748 section 7 are not called for

@ 7.5
> Implementations MUST use the exporter_master_secret unless explicitly specified by the application.
= unused exporters: shioko has no exporter interface and no use for exporter_master_secret; nothing a peer sends can invoke one
> A separate interface for the early exporter is RECOMMENDED; this avoids the exporter user accidentally using an early exporter when a regular one is desired or vice versa.
= unused early exporter: there is no 0-RTT and no exporter interface; nothing a peer sends can invoke one
> Future specifications MUST NOT define a use of exporters that permit both an empty context and no context with the same label.
= info requirement on future specifications that define exporter uses
> New uses of exporters SHOULD provide a context in all exporter computations, though the value could be empty.
= info requirement on specifications that define new uses of exporters; shioko has no exporter interface

@ 8
> Servers SHOULD provide that level of replay safety by implementing one of the methods described in this section or by equivalent means.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> Therefore, in normal operation, clients will not know which, if any, of these mechanisms servers actually implement and hence MUST only send early data which they deem safe to be replayed.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko never accepts early data: its records are skipped, so nothing a client puts in them is delivered, let alone replayed
> The server MUST ensure that any instance of it (be it a machine, a thread, or any other entity within the relevant serving infrastructure) would accept 0-RTT for the same 0-RTT handshake at most once; this limits the number of replays to the number of server instances in the deployment.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> The "at most once per server instance" guarantee is a minimum requirement; servers SHOULD limit 0-RTT replays further when feasible.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> The second class of attack cannot be prevented at the TLS layer and MUST be dealt with by any application.
= info binds the application protocol above TLS (the HTTP client and the upstream service), not the TLS implementation; the design has no step in which shioko itself sends a request a second time (one upstream connection per request, PLAN section 6)

@ 8.2
> Servers MAY also implement data stores with false positives, such as Bloom filters, in which case they MUST respond to apparent replay by rejecting 0-RTT but MUST NOT abort the handshake.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> The server MUST derive the storage key only from validated sections of the ClientHello.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> When implementations are freshly started, they SHOULD reject 0-RTT as long as any portion of their recording window overlaps the startup time.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message

@ 9.1
> A TLS-compliant application MUST implement the TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 [GCM] cipher suite and SHOULD implement the TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 [GCM] and TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256 [RFC8439] cipher suites (see Appendix B.4).
= deviation PLAN section 5, known departures: TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 is not implemented (nor TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384); the one suite is TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, and section 11 accepts that a client offering only AES-GCM cannot connect
> A TLS-compliant application MUST support digital signatures with rsa_pkcs1_sha256 (for certificates), rsa_pss_rsae_sha256 (for CertificateVerify and certificates), and ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256.
= deviation RSA signatures are not implemented; only ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 is, and a site needs an ECDSA P-256 certificate (PLAN 5, known departures)
> A TLS-compliant application MUST support key exchange with secp256r1 (NIST P-256) and SHOULD support key exchange with X25519 [RFC7748].
= deviation PLAN section 5, known departures: key exchange over secp256r1 is not implemented; the one group is X25519 (the SHOULD half), and section 11 accepts that a client offering only secp256r1 cannot connect

@ 9.2
> In the absence of an application profile standard specifying otherwise, a TLS-compliant application MUST implement the following TLS extensions:
= code parse supported_versions, signature_algorithms, supported_groups, key_share and server_name in a ClientHello and act on them as the alert table in PLAN section 5 says
