@ 9.3
> This section describes invariants that TLS endpoints and middleboxes MUST follow.
= info introduces the invariants listed below, each with its own entry
> For this to work, implementations MUST correctly handle extensible fields:
= info introduces the list below, each item with its own entry
> - A client sending a ClientHello MUST support all parameters advertised in it.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko selects only TLS 1.3, TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, x25519 and ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256, and only when the client listed them
> - A server receiving a ClientHello MUST correctly ignore all unrecognized cipher suites, extensions, and other parameters.
= code skip unknown cipher suites, extensions, named groups, key_share entries, versions, signature schemes and PSK modes in a ClientHello without error; only the absence of a required value fails the handshake
= test unknown_values_are_ignored
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
> In TLS 1.3, a client receiving a CertificateRequest or NewSessionTicket MUST also ignore all unrecognized extensions.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko sends neither CertificateRequest nor NewSessionTicket
> - A middlebox which terminates a TLS connection MUST behave as a compliant TLS server (to the original client), including having a certificate which the client is willing to accept, and also as a compliant TLS client (to the original server), including verifying the original server's certificate.
= unused TLS-intercepting middlebox (a TLS client towards an original server): shioko is the TLS endpoint for its own names with their real certificates and speaks plain HTTP to its upstreams (HTTPS upstreams are dropped, PLAN section 2), so it has no TLS client side; its duties as a TLS server are what every other entry accounts for
> In particular, it MUST generate its own ClientHello containing only parameters it understands, and it MUST generate a fresh ServerHello random value, rather than forwarding the endpoint's value.
= unused TLS-intercepting middlebox: shioko sends no ClientHello and has no endpoint ServerHello to forward
= code fill ServerHello.random with 32 fresh bytes from /dev/urandom in every handshake (HelloRetryRequest carries its fixed value)
= test every_server_random_is_new
> - A middlebox which forwards ClientHello parameters it does not understand MUST NOT process any messages beyond that ClientHello.
= unused forwarding TLS without terminating it: every connection on port 443 is terminated; shioko never forwards a ClientHello or any TLS record
> It MUST forward all subsequent traffic unmodified.
= unused forwarding TLS without terminating it: every connection on port 443 is terminated; shioko never forwards a ClientHello or any TLS record

@ 11
> IESG Approval is REQUIRED for a Y->N transition.
= info IANA registry procedure
> IESG Approval is REQUIRED for a Y->N transition.
= info IANA registry procedure

@ A.1
> If clients need to send such alerts, they SHOULD first rekey to the handshake keys if possible.
= peer client: a cleartext alert that arrives after our ServerHello and before the client Finished is taken as the client's alert, as this paragraph allows: the connection ends with nothing said back
= row step wait_finished whole even strict plain:alert => closed whole even strict
= test bug_unencrypted_alert_during_the_handshake_was_answered

@ B
> TLS 1.3 implementations MUST NOT send them but might receive them from older TLS implementations.
= code never send a value marked _RESERVED: every code point we emit is a constant (suite 0x1303, group 0x001D, scheme 0x0403, the alerts in the spec's Alerts set); _RESERVED values in a ClientHello are skipped as unknown
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test unknown_values_are_ignored

@ B.3.1.4
> Values within "obsolete_RESERVED" ranges are used in previous versions of TLS and MUST NOT be offered or negotiated by TLS 1.3 implementations.
= code select group 0x001D only; group ids in the obsolete_RESERVED ranges in supported_groups or key_share are skipped as unknown
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
= test unknown_values_are_ignored

@ C.1
> It is RECOMMENDED to use an existing CSPRNG implementation in preference to crafting a new one.
= code take every random value (ServerHello.random, X25519 private key, ECDSA nonce) from /dev/urandom with no generator of our own, and fail the handshake when the read fails (PLAN sections 4 and 5)
= test random_fills_buffer
= test ecdsa_sign_verifies_and_is_randomised
= test every_server_random_is_new

@ C.2
> Applications SHOULD also enforce minimum and maximum key sizes.
= unused validation of certification paths: shioko validates no certificate (no client certificates); its own key must be P-256 and match the leaf certificate at startup (PLAN section 5)

@ C.3
> It is RECOMMENDED that implementations implement "deterministic ECDSA" as specified in [RFC6979].
= deviation PLAN section 5, known departures: ECDSA nonces are random, read from /dev/urandom for each signature (section 7), not derived as RFC 6979 recommends; the handshake fails if the read fails

@ C.4
> Clients SHOULD NOT reuse a ticket for multiple connections.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko issues no tickets
> Servers that issue tickets SHOULD offer at least as many tickets as the number of connections that a client might use; for example, a web browser using HTTP/1.1 [RFC7230] might open six connections to a server.
= unused session tickets: none are issued (PLAN section 5); a ticket or PSK identity offered by a client is ignored and a full handshake follows
> Servers SHOULD issue new tickets with every connection.
= unused session tickets: the advice is for servers that issue tickets, and shioko issues none (PLAN sections 2 and 5), so there is no ticket for a client to reuse

@ C.5
> If no such mechanism is used, then the connection has no protection against active man-in-the-middle attack; applications MUST NOT use TLS in such a way absent explicit configuration or a specific application profile.
= peer client: nothing to do; whether to accept a server without validating it is the client application's decision, and shioko always authenticates with its certificate chain and CertificateVerify, never with a raw public key

@ D
> The value of TLSPlaintext.legacy_record_version MUST be ignored by all implementations.
= code do not use TLSPlaintext.legacy_record_version for anything: a ClientHello record marked 0x0301, 0x0302, 0x0303, 0x0304 or any other value is processed alike (PLAN section 5: the version field is ignored)
= test bug_record_version_was_not_ignored
= test records_are_framed_and_checked
> In order to maximize interoperability with older endpoints, implementations that negotiate the use of TLS 1.0-1.2 SHOULD set the record layer version number to the negotiated version for the ServerHello and all records thereafter.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer TLS 1.3 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN section 2, change 6)
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> For maximum compatibility with previously non-standard behavior and misconfigured deployments, all implementations SHOULD support validation of certification paths based on the expectations in this document, even when handling prior TLS versions' handshakes (see Section 4.4.2.2).
= unused validation of certification paths, and prior TLS versions: shioko validates no certificate and negotiates no earlier version
> Because TLS 1.3 always hashes in the transcript up to the server Finished, implementations which support both TLS 1.3 and earlier versions SHOULD indicate the use of the Extended Master Secret extension in their APIs whenever TLS 1.3 is used.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: shioko supports TLS 1.3 only and has no API that reports the Extended Master Secret

@ D.1
> A client using a ticket for resumption SHOULD initiate the connection using the version that was previously negotiated.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko issues no tickets
> Note that 0-RTT data is not compatible with older servers and SHOULD NOT be sent absent knowledge that the server supports TLS 1.3.
= peer client: nothing to do; early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> If the version chosen by the server is not supported by the client (or is not acceptable), the client MUST abort the handshake with a "protocol_version" alert.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko only ever answers with TLS 1.3
> Multiple connection attempts may be required in order to negotiate a backward-compatible connection; however, this practice is vulnerable to downgrade attacks and is NOT RECOMMENDED.
= peer client: nothing to do; a fallback ClientHello that no longer offers TLS 1.3 is refused with protocol_version
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version

@ D.2
> If the "supported_versions" extension is present, the server MUST negotiate using that extension as described in Section 4.2.1.
= code when supported_versions is present, decide the version from it alone: TLS 1.3 if it lists 0x0304, otherwise protocol_version; legacy_version plays no part beyond the 0x0300 check of appendix D.5
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test what_a_client_hello_may_offer
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> If the "supported_versions" extension is not present, the server MUST negotiate the minimum of ClientHello.legacy_version and TLS 1.2.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer TLS 1.3 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN section 2, change 6); this is the case of the next entry
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> If the "supported_versions" extension is absent and the server only supports versions greater than ClientHello.legacy_version, the server MUST abort the handshake with a "protocol_version" alert.
= code refuse a ClientHello without supported_versions with protocol_version, whatever its legacy_version
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> Servers will receive various TLS 1.x versions in this field, but its value MUST always be ignored.
= code do not use TLSPlaintext.legacy_record_version for anything: a ClientHello record marked 0x0301, 0x0302, 0x0303, 0x0304 or any other value is processed alike (PLAN section 5: the version field is ignored)
= test bug_record_version_was_not_ignored
= test records_are_framed_and_checked

@ D.3
> A client that attempts to send 0-RTT data MUST fail a connection if it receives a ServerHello with TLS 1.2 or older.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko never sends a ServerHello for TLS 1.2 or older
> To avoid a downgrade attack, the client SHOULD NOT disable TLS 1.3, only 0-RTT.
= peer client: nothing to do
> To avoid this error condition, multi-server deployments SHOULD ensure a uniform and stable deployment of TLS 1.3 without 0-RTT prior to enabling 0-RTT.
= unused 0-RTT: never enabled, so there is no rollout to stage; early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message

@ D.4
> Either side can send change_cipher_spec at any time during the handshake, as they must be ignored by the peer, but if the client sends a non-empty session ID, the server MUST send the change_cipher_spec as described in this appendix.
= row step start whole even strict plain:hello => wait_finished whole even strict send:server_hello send:ccs write_keys:handshake read_keys:handshake send:flight write_keys:data
= row step start whole even strict plain:hello_retry => retry whole even strict send:retry send:ccs
= row step retry whole even strict plain:hello => wait_finished whole even strict send:server_hello write_keys:handshake read_keys:handshake send:flight write_keys:data
= check Handshake.AtMostOnce
= check Handshake.RightKeys
= code send the dummy change_cipher_spec (one unprotected record holding the single byte 0x01) right after the first of ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest, whether or not the client's session id was empty
= test a_hello_retry_request_and_then_the_handshake
= test same_as_the_spec_on_every_input
= test what_our_first_records_look_like
= deviation PLAN section 5, known departures, on the clause that the peer ignores change_cipher_spec: one that arrives between the pieces of a split handshake message is refused with unexpected_message, not dropped (step retry partial even strict plain:ccs => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message)

@ D.5
> Implementations negotiating the use of older versions of TLS SHOULD prefer forward secret and AEAD cipher suites, when available.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; a ClientHello that does not offer TLS 1.3 is refused with protocol_version (PLAN section 2, change 6)
> Implementations MUST NOT offer or negotiate RC4 cipher suites for any version of TLS for any reason.
= code ServerHello.cipher_suite is always 0x1303; RC4 suites in a ClientHello are skipped as unknown
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
> Ciphers with a strength less than 112 bits MUST NOT be offered or negotiated for any version of TLS for any reason.
= code ServerHello.cipher_suite is always 0x1303; weaker suites in a ClientHello are skipped as unknown
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
> The security of SSL 3.0 [RFC6101] is considered insufficient for the reasons enumerated in [RFC7568], and it MUST NOT be negotiated for any reason.
= code never negotiate any version but TLS 1.3 (ServerHello legacy_version 0x0303 with supported_versions 0x0304); an SSL 3.0 ClientHello is refused with protocol_version
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
= test what_is_refused_and_with_which_alert
= test bug_ssl3_legacy_version_was_served
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> The security of SSL 2.0 [SSL2] is considered insufficient for the reasons enumerated in [RFC6176], and it MUST NOT be negotiated for any reason.
= code never negotiate any version but TLS 1.3; an SSL 2.0 CLIENT-HELLO is not a TLS record and fails as a record of unknown type with unexpected_message
= test bug_unknown_record_type_got_decode_error
= test records_are_framed_and_checked
= row step start whole even strict invalid:unexpected_message => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> Implementations MUST NOT send an SSL version 2.0 compatible CLIENT-HELLO.
= unused TLS client role: shioko never sends a ClientHello of any kind; its upstreams are plain HTTP
> Implementations MUST NOT negotiate TLS 1.3 or later using an SSL version 2.0 compatible CLIENT-HELLO.
= code treat an SSL 2.0 format CLIENT-HELLO as an invalid first record (unknown content type, unexpected_message) and never parse it as a ClientHello
= test records_are_framed_and_checked
= row step start whole even strict invalid:unexpected_message => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> Implementations are NOT RECOMMENDED to accept an SSL version 2.0 compatible CLIENT-HELLO in order to negotiate older versions of TLS.
= unused TLS 1.2 and earlier: never negotiated; an SSL 2.0 format CLIENT-HELLO is refused as an invalid first record
= row step start whole even strict invalid:unexpected_message => closed whole even strict alert:unexpected_message
> Implementations MUST NOT send a ClientHello.legacy_version or ServerHello.legacy_version set to 0x0300 or less.
= code set legacy_version to 0x0303 in ServerHello and in HelloRetryRequest
= test our_hellos_field_by_field
> Any endpoint receiving a Hello message with ClientHello.legacy_version or ServerHello.legacy_version set to 0x0300 MUST abort the handshake with a "protocol_version" alert.
= code refuse a ClientHello whose legacy_version is 0x0300 with protocol_version, even when supported_versions lists 0x0304
= test bug_ssl3_legacy_version_was_served
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> Implementations MUST NOT send any records with a version less than 0x0300.
= code write legacy_record_version 0x0303 on every record we send
= test sealed_records_open_with_the_same_keys_and_sequence_only
= test what_our_first_records_look_like
> Implementations SHOULD NOT accept any records with a version less than 0x0300 (but may inadvertently do so if the record version number is ignored completely).
= code ignore legacy_record_version completely, as section 5.1 requires, so that a record below 0x0300 is accepted like any other (which this sentence allows); a ClientHello whose legacy_version is 0x0300 or lower is refused with protocol_version (PLAN section 5)
= test bug_record_version_was_not_ignored
= row step start whole even strict invalid:protocol_version => closed whole even strict alert:protocol_version
> Implementations MUST NOT use the Truncated HMAC extension, defined in Section 7 of [RFC6066], as it is not applicable to AEAD algorithms and has been shown to be insecure in some scenarios.
= unused truncated_hmac: never negotiated; the extension in a ClientHello is skipped as unknown and never echoed in EncryptedExtensions

@ E.1
> Implementations MUST NOT combine external PSKs with certificate-based authentication of either the client or the server unless negotiated by some extension.
= unused PSK, external or from a ticket: no PSK is ever selected, so authentication is by certificate alone
> Note: Exporters can produce arbitrary-length values; if exporters are to be used as channel bindings, the exported value MUST be large enough to provide collision resistance.
= unused exporters: shioko has no exporter interface and no use for exporter_master_secret; nothing a peer sends can invoke one

@ E.1.1
> This ordering should always be followed (including in future revisions of this document); in particular, one SHOULD NOT use an output of HKDF-Extract as an input to another application of HKDF-Extract without an HKDF-Expand in between.
= info advice to the designers of this and of future key schedules
= code derive the key schedule exactly as section 7.1, with Derive-Secret(., "derived", "") between successive HKDF-Extract steps, and check it against the RFC 8448 trace
= test the_simple_handshake_of_rfc8448
= test the_hello_retry_request_of_rfc8448

@ E.1.4
> However, because these secrets can be used to compute any exporter value, they SHOULD be erased as soon as possible.
= unused exporters: shioko has no exporter interface, so exporter_master_secret need not be derived at all and nothing is kept
> If the total set of exporter labels is known, then implementations SHOULD pre-compute the inner Derive-Secret stage of the exporter computation for all those labels, then erase the [early_]exporter_master_secret, followed by each inner value as soon as it is known that it will not be needed again.
= unused exporters: there are no exporter labels and so nothing to pre-compute or erase

@ E.5
> Because clients do not know the exact details of server behavior, they MUST NOT send messages in early data which are not safe to have replayed and which they would not be willing to retry across multiple 1-RTT connections.
= peer client: nothing to do; shioko never accepts early data: its records are skipped, so nothing a client puts in them is delivered, let alone replayed
> Application protocols MUST NOT use 0-RTT data without a profile that defines its use.
= unused 0-RTT: early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
> In addition, to avoid accidental misuse, TLS implementations MUST NOT enable 0-RTT (either sending or accepting) unless specifically requested by the application and MUST NOT automatically resend 0-RTT data if it is rejected by the server unless instructed by the application.
= unused 0-RTT: never enabled in either direction (PLAN sections 2 and 5); early data is never accepted (PLAN section 5): offered PSKs are ignored, a full handshake follows, and early-data records are skipped as RFC 8446 4.2.10 requires, up to 16 KB, after which the connection ends with unexpected_message
= row step wait_finished whole even skipping undecryptable:- => wait_finished whole even skipping skip:-
= row step wait_finished whole even skipping invalid:bad_record_mac => closed whole even strict alert:bad_record_mac
= check Handshake.SkipOnlyEarlyData
> In order to allow applications to implement this kind of processing, TLS implementations MUST provide a way for the application to determine if the handshake has completed.
= check Handshake.DataOnlyAfterFinished
= row step wait_finished whole even strict write:- => wait_finished whole even strict
= code hand the TLS stream to the HTTP layer only once the client Finished has verified, so the application never holds a connection whose handshake is incomplete
= test a_wrong_finished_is_decrypt_error
= test a_whole_handshake_and_data_both_ways

@ E.5.1
> In addition, the early exporter SHOULD NOT be used to generate server-to-client encryption keys because that would entail the reuse of those keys.
= unused early exporter: there is no 0-RTT and no exporter interface
